<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_fleqn">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Matan Avitan<a href="https://orcid.org/0009-0001-0762-9616" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href"></a>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Bar-Ilan University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Moran Baruch<a href="https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0615-6164" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href"></a>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Nir Drucker<a href="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7273-4797" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href"></a>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Itamar Zimerman<a href="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8321-0609" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href"></a>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Tel Aviv University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yoav Goldberg<a href="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6497-829X" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href"></a>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Allen Institute for Artificial Intelligence
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author"><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>IBM Research
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) power modern AI applications, but processing sensitive data on untrusted servers raises privacy concerns. Homomorphic encryption (HE) enables computation on encrypted data for secure inference. However, neural text generation requires decoding methods like <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>sampling</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are non-polynomial and thus computationally expensive under encryption, creating a significant performance bottleneck.
We introduce <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math>, an HE-friendly <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> algorithm that reduces ciphertext operations compared to prior methods, enabling practical greedy decoding under encryption. We also propose the first HE-compatible nucleus (top-<math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) sampling method, leveraging <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> for efficient stochastic decoding with provable privacy guarantees. Both techniques are polynomial, supporting efficient inference in privacy-preserving settings. Moreover, their differentiability facilitates gradient-based sequence-level optimization as a polynomial alternative to straight-through estimators. We further provide strong theoretical guarantees for <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math>, proving it converges globally to a unique two-level fixed point, independent of the input values beyond the identity of the maximizer, which explains its rapid convergence in just a few iterations. Evaluations on realistic LLM outputs show latency reductions of <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="24\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24\times</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="35\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>35</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35\times</annotation></semantics></math> over baselines, advancing secure text generation.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;">
</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08383v1/imgs/motivation-generation-enc-logits.jpg" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:457/182;" width="457" height="182" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S0.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scope</span>: Secure LLM generation over the clients’ encrypted data using HE (model is not necessarily encrypted). Here, standard decoding methods like <math id="S0.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S0.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sampling</span> (<span id="S0.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">red</span>) for selecting the next token are not HE-friendly or considered inefficient. We introduce efficient and scalable HE-friendly ways to evaluate them under encryption. </figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in LLMs have enabled the development of powerful AI systems capable of generating fluent text at scale <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. However, deploying these models in real-world applications often involves sending sensitive user data, such as personal messages or medical records, to remote servers, raising significant privacy concerns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Homomorphic encryption (HE) offers a promising solution by allowing computations on encrypted data, ensuring that servers can process queries without accessing their plaintext content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gentry, 2009</a>)</cite>. Under HE, users encrypt their inputs, the server performs computations (e.g., running an LLM), and returns an encrypted result that only the user can decrypt.
In this paper, we present differentiable and polynomial <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{nucleus}\thinspace\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>nucleus</mi><mo lspace="0.337em">⁡</mo><mi>sampling</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{nucleus}\thinspace\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math> algorithms tailored for encrypted LLM decoding. Unlike prior approaches, such as that of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bengio et al. (2013)</a></cite>, which use <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> in the forward pass and estimate gradients in the backward pass via straight-through estimators (STE), our methods are fully differentiable in their plaintext form (without approximations) and remain so under polynomial HE approximations. This differentiability, together with their polynomial form, makes them well suited to AI-privacy settings such as HE; the plaintext formulations are exactly differentiable, and the HE instantiations preserve differentiability via polynomial approximations (see <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 HE-Friendly CutMax ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sect.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).
Most HE schemes, including the CKKS scheme used in this work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheon et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, support only polynomial operations like addition and multiplication. This limitation necessitates the design of <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HE-friendly</span> algorithms that rely exclusively on such operations, posing a challenge for text generation tasks that involve complex decoding steps. While secure inference on LLMs using HE has been demonstrated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zimerman et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">de Castro et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, extending these techniques to multi-token text generation remains challenging. Decoding methods like <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> (for greedy decoding) and <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>sampling</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., nucleus sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holtzman et al., 2019a</a>)</cite>) are computationally inefficient or impractical in the encrypted domain due to their reliance on non-polynomial operations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">State-of-the-art homomorphic <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> implementations follow two comparison-heavy patterns: a <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tournament</em> tree and an all-to-all <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">league</em> schedule, both realized via deep polynomial approximations of <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chakraborty and Zuber, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Crawford et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Folkerts and Tsoutsos, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jovanovic et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
A detailed illustration of these designs is provided in <a href="#A3.F5" title="In Appendix C Previous, Comparison-Based Methods for argmax over HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (<a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Previous, Comparison-Based Methods for argmax over HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>), which highlights their sequential comparison stages and reliance on costly <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> evaluations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> removes comparisons altogether. Each of its <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> iterations applies two global reductions (mean and variance), an inverse-square-root (<math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{InvSqr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>InvSqr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{InvSqr}</annotation></semantics></math>), an elementwise odd power, and a final normalization (<a href="#S3" title="3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sect.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.1 The CutMax Algorithm ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 HE-Friendly CutMax ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sect.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, <a href="#alg2" title="In 3.2 HE-Friendly CutMax ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Consequently, the number of <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sequential</em> stages is a small constant <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>—<em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weakly dependent</em> on the vector length <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., the vocabulary size)—whereas tournament and league require <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{2}n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{2}n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> sequential rounds, respectively, each performing encrypted comparisons via polynomial approximations of <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span>. Because <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{InvSqr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>InvSqr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{InvSqr}</annotation></semantics></math> and division (<math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Inv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Inv</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Inv}</annotation></semantics></math>) are shallower and faster than <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> at similar accuracy (<a href="#A2.T3" title="In Multiplication Depth. ‣ B.1 Goldschmidt ‣ Appendix B Approximations for HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), <math id="S1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves much lower depth and fewer bootstraps per token. Empirically, <math id="S1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a small constant (e.g., <math id="S1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{\leq}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{\leq}3</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>), consistent with the latency gains we observe for vocabularies of <math id="S1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}|\!\approx\!3.3\times 10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em">≈</mo><mrow><mn>3.3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}|\!\approx\!3.3\times 10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.5\times 10^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5\times 10^{5}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S4" title="4 Empirical Evaluation ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sect.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <a href="#S4.T2" title="In 4.2 HE CutMax ‣ 4 Empirical Evaluation ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Implementation-level costs such as rotations under SIMD packing are deferred to <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 HE-Friendly CutMax ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sect.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Argmax using SIMD ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions address key bottlenecks in secure text generation
, as illustrated in <a href="#S0.F1" title="In Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, and include:
(i) <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CutMax</span>. An efficient, <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">homomorphic encryption (HE)</span>-friendly <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> algorithm that enables practical greedy decoding under encryption with fewer ciphertext operations than previous methods.
(ii) <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Encrypted nucleus sampling</span>. The first <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span>-compatible <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{nucleus}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>nucleus</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{nucleus}</annotation></semantics></math> (top-<math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>sampling</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math> method, enables stochastic decoding with provable privacy guarantees, via <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(iii) <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theoretical convergence guarantees</span>. A formal proof establishing that <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">converges globally to a unique two-level fixed point</span>, providing a rigorous foundation for its rapid convergence in a small, constant number of iterations.
(iv) <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Differentiable decoding primitives</span>. Implementations of <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{nucleus}\thinspace\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>nucleus</mi><mo lspace="0.337em">⁡</mo><mi>sampling</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{nucleus}\thinspace\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math> that are real-analytic and fully differentiable in plaintext, leveraging smooth operations like <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{x}</annotation></semantics></math> (and polynomial-only in their HE approximations) to enable exact gradient-based sequence-level training as a theoretically grounded alternative to straight-through estimators.
By enabling efficient and accurate multi-token generation under full encryption, our work advances the deployment of privacy-preserving LLMs in real-world settings, bridging a critical gap in secure AI systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background: Homomorphic Argmax</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">State-of-the-art homomorphic <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> implementations for LLM decoding rely on comparison-heavy designs, primarily the <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tournament</em> tree and <em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">league</em> schedule, both implemented via deep polynomial approximations of the <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chakraborty and Zuber, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Crawford et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Folkerts and Tsoutsos, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jovanovic et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. In the tournament design, approximately <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{-}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{-}1</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise comparisons are organized into <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{2}n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{2}n</annotation></semantics></math> sequential stages, halving the number of candidates per stage until the maximum remains. The league design performs <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{2}=\Theta(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{2}=\Theta(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> comparisons across <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> sequential rounds, accumulating scores such that the maximum achieves a score of <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{-}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{-}1</annotation></semantics></math>. Both methods are illustrated in <a href="#A3.F5" title="In Appendix C Previous, Comparison-Based Methods for argmax over HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (<a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Previous, Comparison-Based Methods for argmax over HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>). Despite leveraging CKKS SIMD parallelism to execute multiple comparisons within a round, these approaches incur high multiplicative depth due to repeated <span id="S2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> evaluations, leading to costly bootstrapping and limiting scalability for large vocabularies (<math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\sim 10^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\sim 10^{5}</annotation></semantics></math>). Some works, such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chakraborty and Zuber (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Folkerts and Tsoutsos (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grivet Sébert et al. (2021)</a></cite>, use TFHE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chillotti et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, restricting input sizes (e.g., <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 256</annotation></semantics></math>) due to efficiency constraints. Hybrid approaches combine tournament and league methods to exploit SIMD packing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iliashenko and Zucca, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazzone et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, but still rely on slow <span id="S2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> approximations. As shown in <a href="#A2.T3" title="In Multiplication Depth. ‣ B.1 Goldschmidt ‣ Appendix B Approximations for HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Approximations for HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>), <span id="S2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> evaluations are significantly deeper and slower than <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{inverse}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>inverse</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{inverse}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{inverse-square-root}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>inverse</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>square</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>root</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{inverse-square-root}</annotation></semantics></math> operations, which our <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> algorithm uses instead (<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 HE-Friendly CutMax ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sect.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>). These limitations make prior methods impractical for efficient, privacy-preserving LLM decoding, motivating our polynomial-based approach that eliminates comparisons entirely.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>HE Preliminaries</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span> enables computation on encrypted data without requiring decryption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gentry (2009)</a></cite>. Informally, it is defined as follows: Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{1}(+,\cdot),\mathcal{R}_{2}(\oplus,\odot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊕</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊙</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{1}(+,\cdot),\mathcal{R}_{2}(\oplus,\odot)</annotation></semantics></math> be two rings for the plaintext and ciphertext spaces, respectively. Given plaintext inputs <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1},m_{2}\in\mathcal{R}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1},m_{2}\in\mathcal{R}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, their encryption <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\![m_{i}]\!]:=\text{Enc}(m_{i})\in\mathcal{R}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mtext>Enc</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\![m_{i}]\!]:=\text{Enc}(m_{i})\in\mathcal{R}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfies the following correctness and homomorphic properties:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Dec}([\![m_{1}]\!])=m_{1}+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Dec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Dec}([\![m_{1}]\!])=m_{1}+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:1.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Dec}([\![m_{1}]\!]\oplus[\![m_{2}]\!])=m_{1}+m_{2}+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Dec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Dec}([\![m_{1}]\!]\oplus[\![m_{2}]\!])=m_{1}+m_{2}+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:1.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Dec}([\![m_{1}]\!]\odot[\![m_{2}]\!])=m_{1}\cdot m_{2}+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Dec</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Dec}([\![m_{1}]\!]\odot[\![m_{2}]\!])=m_{1}\cdot m_{2}+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a small noise introduced by the <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span> scheme, similar to the noise accumulated in floating-point computations. <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span> is <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semantically secure</span> and thus every call to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Enc(m_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Enc(m_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> results with a new pseudo random ciphertext.
Details about the standard <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">threat-model</span> of <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span>-based applications are provided in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Threat model ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.
As <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span> only supports <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">polynomial operations</span> (additions and multiplications), standard transformer components like <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{softmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{softmax}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{layer\ normalization}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>layer</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>normalization</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{layer\ normalization}</annotation></semantics></math> are not directly supported and must be approximated or replaced. Prior-art such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gilad-Bachrach et al. (2016)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aharoni et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baruch et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baruch et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zimerman et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zimerman et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">de Castro et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024a)</a></cite> have proposed polynomial-friendly adaptations for encrypted inference.
We focus on the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generation process</span> where new challenges arise: decoding operations such as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>sampling</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math> are not polynomial.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents our polynomial algorithms for <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{nucleus}\thinspace\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>nucleus</mi><mo lspace="0.337em">⁡</mo><mi>sampling</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{nucleus}\thinspace\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math>, tailored for <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>The CutMax Algorithm</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> repeatedly ‘stretches’ the distribution of values and cuts off the lower part, such that after a few iterations only the highest value remains significantly non-zero (see <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.1 The CutMax Algorithm ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> is inspired by standardization in statistics (subtracting the mean and dividing by standard deviation), alongside the idea of centering the array around 1; therefore, when taking an odd power of the standardized values, values smaller than the mean would vanish while values greater than the mean would amplify.
The algorithm’s fast convergence is due to the right skewness induced by the power operation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> <math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> </figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=(X_{1},\dots,X_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(X_{1},\dots,X_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,T\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,T\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> - one-hot approx. of <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>argmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{(1)}=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{(1)}=X</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}Y^{(t)}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}Y^{(t)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Mean of <math id="alg1.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{2}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(Y^{(t)}_{i}-\mu)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{2}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(Y^{(t)}_{i}-\mu)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Variance of <math id="alg1.l6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{(t+1)}=\left(\frac{Y^{(t)}_{i}-\mu}{c\cdot\sqrt{s^{2}}}+1\right)_{i\in[n]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msqrt><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{(t+1)}=\left(\frac{Y^{(t)}_{i}-\mu}{c\cdot\sqrt{s^{2}}}+1\right)_{i\in[n]}</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Standardization
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{(t+1)}=\left(Y^{(t+1)}\right)^{p}_{i\in[n]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{(t+1)}=\left(Y^{(t+1)}\right)^{p}_{i\in[n]}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\left(\frac{Y^{(T)}_{i}}{\Sigma_{j}{Y^{(T)}_{j}}}\right)_{i\in[n]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\left(\frac{Y^{(T)}_{i}}{\Sigma_{j}{Y^{(T)}_{j}}}\right)_{i\in[n]}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#alg1" title="In 3.1 The CutMax Algorithm ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides the pseudo-code for <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math>, which takes as input a vector <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> elements, an odd integer <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, a constant scaling factor <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the maximum number of iterations <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, one instantiation uses <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=11</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=5</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=3</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the index set as <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]=\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]=\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> is an iterative algorithm that runs for at most <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> iterations or until convergence. Each iteration <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> consists of two steps: <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">standardization</span> and <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">distance amplification</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08383v1/histogram_cutmax_convergence.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/100;" width="476" height="100"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>
<span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Illustration of CutMax First Iteration on a Normal Distribution</span>
<math id="S3.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> algorithm first iteration illustration, applied to the original random variable <math id="S3.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim{}\mathrm{Normal}(170,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Normal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>170</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim{}\mathrm{Normal}(170,10)</annotation></semantics></math> (a). We first standardize and shift <math id="S3.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="S3.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\frac{X-\mu_{X}}{c\sigma_{X}}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\frac{X-\mu_{X}}{c\sigma_{X}}+1</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=5</annotation></semantics></math> (b), then raise <math id="S3.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> to the power <math id="S3.F2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=3</annotation></semantics></math> (c) or <math id="S3.F2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=7</annotation></semantics></math> (d) to induce right skew. The resulting distribution <math id="S3.F2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=Y^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=Y^{7}</annotation></semantics></math> (d) has a maximum normalized height
<math id="S3.F2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\bigl(Z/\sum_{i}Z_{i}\bigr)=0.0033" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0033</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\bigl(Z/\sum_{i}Z_{i}\bigr)=0.0033</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating a strong right-tail skew. A vertical black line indicates the mean of every distribution and gray lines indicate the first standard deviation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Standardization (Lines 3-5).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The normalization process starts by computing the mean <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> of the current vector state <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, the variance <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the standard deviation <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=\sqrt{s^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=\sqrt{s^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Subsequently, it scales <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> by subtracting the mean and dividing by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\cdot\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\cdot\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. The choice of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> controls how much we “flatten” the distribution before raising the power. A larger <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> means we divide by a larger number, making the normalized values smaller in magnitude.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Distance Amplification (Line 6).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We raise each normalized element to an odd power <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, which preserves the sign of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}-\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}-\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.
Elements above (resp. below) the mean (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}-\mu&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}-\mu&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) remain positive (resp. negative).
The effect of this power raising is twofold: it shrinks the magnitude of small values and amplifies the larger values, where</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left|Y^{(t)}_{i}\right|&lt;1\Longrightarrow\left|Y^{(t)}_{i}\right|\gg\left|Y^{(t)}_{i}\right|^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≫</mo><msup><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left|Y^{(t)}_{i}\right|&lt;1\Longrightarrow\left|Y^{(t)}_{i}\right|\gg\left|Y^{(t)}_{i}\right|^{p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left|Y^{(t)}_{i}\right|&gt;1\Longrightarrow\left|Y^{(t)}_{i}\right|\ll\left|Y^{(t)}_{i}\right|^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≪</mo><msup><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left|Y^{(t)}_{i}\right|&gt;1\Longrightarrow\left|Y^{(t)}_{i}\right|\ll\left|Y^{(t)}_{i}\right|^{p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">By appropriately scaling <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> before this step, we ensure that all but the largest elements satisfy <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|Y_{i}|&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|Y_{i}|&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, causing them to shrink, while the largest (and possibly a few near-largest) elements may have <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|Y_{i}|&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|Y_{i}|&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and thus grow. This drastically amplifies the gap between the maximum and the rest.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Convergence.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid convergence of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> is guaranteed by its behavior as a dynamical system.
As formally proven in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>, one
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> inner update followed by normalization strictly reduces the dispersion
among all non-maximum entries in <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">standardized</em> space, driving them toward
their common mean. In particular, the non-top residual dispersion contracts
geometrically by an explicit factor <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> (see (<a href="#A5.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Step 4: global convergence to the two–level fixed point. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>)) whenever
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;\sqrt{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;\sqrt{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, any initial vector with a unique maximum evolves
toward a two–level state in which all non-maximum elements are equal, and the
normalized iterates converge to a <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unique two-level</em> fixed point that
depends only on <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,c,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,c,p)</annotation></semantics></math>—independent of the input beyond the identity of its
maximal coordinate (cf. <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem E.1 (CutMax convergence). ‣ Step 4: global convergence to the two–level fixed point. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond this theoretical contraction, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> also prunes much more aggressively
than comparison-based schemes. Unlike the tournament <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math>, which discards only
half of the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{Y}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{Y}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> values per stage, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> zeroes out a <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">large majority</em>
each round: empirically, after iteration <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the empirical CDF at the mean
satisfies <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CDF}_{Y^{(t)}}(\mu^{(t)})\gg\tfrac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>CDF</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≫</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CDF}_{Y^{(t)}}(\mu^{(t)})\gg\tfrac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so far more
than half of the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{(t)}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{(t)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> fall below the mean and are driven toward zero by
the subsequent odd-power step (<a href="#S3.F3" title="In Convergence. ‣ 3.1 The CutMax Algorithm ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As established by <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem E.1 (CutMax convergence). ‣ Step 4: global convergence to the two–level fixed point. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>, these properties guarantee
global convergence to the fixed point under mild conditions; for the practical
hyperparameters we use, the limit is nearly one-hot. This explains why only a
small, nearly constant number of iterations (e.g., <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\!\leq\!3\text{\textendash}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">≤</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>–</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\!\leq\!3\text{\textendash}4</annotation></semantics></math>) suffices
in practice, regardless of the initial logit distribution (<a href="#S4" title="4 Empirical Evaluation ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sect.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08383v1/cutmax_convergence_T_3_best_hp_1000_samples.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:433/325;" width="433" height="325"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evolution of Logits Below the Mean Across CutMax Iterations</span> (After <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.1 The CutMax Algorithm ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#alg1.l7" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.1 The CutMax Algorithm ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). We ran CutMax for <math id="S3.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=3</annotation></semantics></math> with the best hyperparameters <math id="S3.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,c)=(19,27)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>19</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>27</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,c)=(19,27)</annotation></semantics></math>, found via grid search, on 1,000 arXiv articles passed through GPT-2 (vocabulary size <math id="S3.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}|=50{,}257" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>50,257</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}|=50{,}257</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2019</a>)</cite>). Bars show the mean<math id="S3.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>std of the fraction of entries <math id="S3.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{Y}^{(t)}_{i}&lt;\mathbb{E}[{Y}^{(t)}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{Y}^{(t)}_{i}&lt;\mathbb{E}[{Y}^{(t)}]</annotation></semantics></math> at each iteration <math id="S3.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>HE-Friendly CutMax</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on the HE preliminaries in <a href="#S2" title="2 Background: Homomorphic Argmax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sect.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we adapt <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> to be fully polynomial by approximating <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (Line 5, <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.1 The CutMax Algorithm ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sum_{j}Y^{(T)}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sum_{j}Y^{(T)}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (Line 7) using Goldschmidt methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldschmidt (1964)</a></cite> (details in <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Goldschmidt ‣ Appendix B Approximations for HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sect.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>). This yields <a href="#alg2" title="In 3.2 HE-Friendly CutMax ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the FHE variant.
Notably, having a polynomial algorithm is not enough, we also need to ensure that no overflows or precision issues compromise the correctness of the results. Specifically, we must prove the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(i) No overflows.</span> Let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\text{CKKS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mtext>CKKS</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\text{CKKS}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the bound configured by the scheme on the unencrypted inputs, beyond which an overflow may occur. Then, all intermediate values produced by the algorithm must remain below <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\text{CKKS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mtext>CKKS</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\text{CKKS}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(ii) No approximation errors.</span> The <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{InvSqr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>InvSqr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{InvSqr}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Inv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Inv</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Inv}</annotation></semantics></math> approximations assume inputs lie within a certain range, e.g., <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\frac{1}{2^{n}},1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\frac{1}{2^{n}},1]</annotation></semantics></math> for some small <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. If an input <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> falls in a wider range <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math>, we instead compute <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\operatorname{InvSqr}(\frac{x}{b})}{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>InvSqr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>b</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\operatorname{InvSqr}(\frac{x}{b})}{b}</annotation></semantics></math>. We must show that <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a}{b}&gt;\frac{1}{2^{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a}{b}&gt;\frac{1}{2^{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise, the approximation may yield incorrect results.
<a href="#S2" title="2 Background: Homomorphic Argmax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sect.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reviewed the tournament method that requires <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n)</annotation></semantics></math> comparisons in <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log n</annotation></semantics></math> sequential stages and the league method that requires <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> comparisons in <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> sequential stages. In contrast, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> requires only <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{InvSqr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>InvSqr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{InvSqr}</annotation></semantics></math> and 1 <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Inv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Inv</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Inv}</annotation></semantics></math> operations.
<a href="#A2.T3" title="In Multiplication Depth. ‣ B.1 Goldschmidt ‣ Appendix B Approximations for HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Approximations for HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> (taken from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moon et al. (2024a)</a></cite>) shows that the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{InvSqr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>InvSqr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{InvSqr}</annotation></semantics></math> implementation is <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.5\text{--}2.1\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mtext>–</mtext><mn>2.1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5\text{--}2.1\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster than the <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> implementation used in all prior art, at the same error level.
In addition, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is weakly-dependent on <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and much smaller. Consequently, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> outperforms prior-art.
Unfortunately, the situation is not that simple. Modern <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span> schemes support <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">single instruction multiple data (SIMD)</span> operations, meaning a ciphertext can encrypt a vector of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> elements instead of just a single element. In this case, multiplications (<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math>), additions (<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math>), and subtractions (<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ominus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊖</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ominus</annotation></semantics></math>) are applied element-wise homomorphically over the vector. Furthermore, a homomorphic rotation by <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&lt;s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&lt;s</annotation></semantics></math> positions is also available <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheon et al., 2017</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> <math id="alg2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMaxHE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMaxHE</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMaxHE}</annotation></semantics></math> - Encrypted <math id="alg2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> </figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
            <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\![X]\!]=[\![(X_{1},\dots,X_{n})]\!]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\![X]\!]=[\![(X_{1},\dots,X_{n})]\!]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,T\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,T\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="alg2.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, and <math id="alg2.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
            <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\![Z]\!]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Z</mi><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\![Z]\!]</annotation></semantics></math> one-hot approx. of <math id="alg2.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>argmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
              
            <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\![Y^{(1)}]\!]=[\![X]\!]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\![Y^{(1)}]\!]=[\![X]\!]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg2.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\![\mu]\!]=\frac{1}{n}\operatorname{RotAndSum}([\![Y^{(t)}]\!])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>μ</mi><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>RotAndSum</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\![\mu]\!]=\frac{1}{n}\operatorname{RotAndSum}([\![Y^{(t)}]\!])</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\![s^{2}]\!]=\frac{1}{n}\operatorname{RotAndSum}\left(([\![Y^{(t)}]\!]\ominus[\![\mu]\!])^{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>RotAndSum</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>μ</mi><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\![s^{2}]\!]=\frac{1}{n}\operatorname{RotAndSum}\left(([\![Y^{(t)}]\!]\ominus[\![\mu]\!])^{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>


</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\![\sigma^{-1}]\!]=\operatorname{InvSqr}([\![s^{2}]\!])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>InvSqr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\![\sigma^{-1}]\!]=\operatorname{InvSqr}([\![s^{2}]\!])</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\![Y^{(t+1)}]\!]=\left(\frac{1}{c}[\![\sigma^{-1}]\!]\odot([\![Y^{(t)}]\!]\ominus[\![\mu]\!])\right)\oplus{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>μ</mi><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\![Y^{(t+1)}]\!]=\left(\frac{1}{c}[\![\sigma^{-1}]\!]\odot([\![Y^{(t)}]\!]\ominus[\![\mu]\!])\right)\oplus{1}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\![Y^{(t+1)}]\!]=\left(([\![Y^{(t+1)}]\!]\right)^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\![Y^{(t+1)}]\!]=\left(([\![Y^{(t+1)}]\!]\right)^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
              
              
            <math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\![Z]\!]=[\![Y^{(T)}]\!]\odot\operatorname{Inv}\left(\operatorname{RotAndSum}([\![Y^{(T)}]\!])\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Z</mi><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mi>Inv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>RotAndSum</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\![Z]\!]=[\![Y^{(T)}]\!]\odot\operatorname{Inv}\left(\operatorname{RotAndSum}([\![Y^{(T)}]\!])\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg2.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\![Z]\!]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Z</mi><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\![Z]\!]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We defer the discussion of how <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">SIMD</span> impacts latency to <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Argmax using SIMD ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>, and here we complete the picture with an implementation of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> over <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span>. <a href="#alg2" title="In 3.2 HE-Friendly CutMax ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> is the <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span> approximation variant of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the input vector <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is encrypted. For brevity, we assume <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=a\cdot s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=a\cdot s</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, and write the algorithm so that modern compilers such as HELayers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aharoni et al. (2023)</a></cite> can automatically implement it.
Lines 3, 4, and 8 of <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.1 The CutMax Algorithm ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> are efficiently computed in <a href="#alg2" title="In 3.2 HE-Friendly CutMax ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> using the <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{RotAndSum}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>RotAndSum</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{RotAndSum}</annotation></semantics></math> method (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Adir et al. (2024)</a></cite>), which takes an encrypted vector <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=(v_{1},\ldots,v_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=(v_{1},\ldots,v_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> and returns a vector of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> elements, each containing the sum <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. This requires <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log s</annotation></semantics></math> rotations and additions.
Multiplying by a plaintext scalar (<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{n}</annotation></semantics></math> at lines 3,4 and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{c}</annotation></semantics></math> at line 6) is considered a cheap <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span> operation as well as the homomorphic subtractions at lines 4 and 6. Raising to the power of 2 (Line 4) and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> (Line 6) require 1 and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p</annotation></semantics></math> multiplications, respectively. Finally, the heaviest operations used are the <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{InvSqr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>InvSqr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{InvSqr}</annotation></semantics></math> (Line 5) and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Inv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Inv</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Inv}</annotation></semantics></math> (Line 8) that use the Goldschmidt approximations (<a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Goldschmidt ‣ Appendix B Approximations for HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sect.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>).
Denote by <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\operatorname{Inv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Inv</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\operatorname{Inv}}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\operatorname{Inv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>Inv</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\operatorname{Inv}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\operatorname{InvSqr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>InvSqr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\operatorname{InvSqr}}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\operatorname{InvSqr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>InvSqr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\operatorname{InvSqr}}</annotation></semantics></math>) the number of multiplications (resp. multiplication depth) required by the <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Inv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Inv</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Inv}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{InvSqr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>InvSqr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{InvSqr}</annotation></semantics></math> approximations over one ciphertext, respectively. Then <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMaxHE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMaxHE</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMaxHE}</annotation></semantics></math> executes</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\cdot(M_{\operatorname{InvSqr}}+5+\log p)+M_{\operatorname{Inv}}+1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>InvSqr</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Inv</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\cdot(M_{\operatorname{InvSqr}}+5+\log p)+M_{\operatorname{Inv}}+1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">multiplications with a total depth of</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\cdot(D_{\operatorname{InvSqr}}+5+\log p)+D_{\operatorname{Inv}}+1\," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>InvSqr</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>Inv</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\cdot(D_{\operatorname{InvSqr}}+5+\log p)+D_{\operatorname{Inv}}+1\,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T\log s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T\log s)</annotation></semantics></math> rotations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>HE-Friendly Nucleus Sampling</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In text generation, language models produce a probability distribution over a large vocabulary at each time step. The next token is selected from this distribution using a decoding strategy. The simplest strategy is greedy decoding, where the token with the highest probability (<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math>) is chosen. More commonly, however, high-quality text generation relies on stochastic decoding methods that introduce controlled randomness to improve fluency, diversity, and coherence.
Two widely used stochastic decoding methods are <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{top-k}\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>top</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>sampling</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{top-k}\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al. (2018)</a></cite> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{nucleus}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>nucleus</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{nucleus}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(top-p)</span> <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>sampling</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holtzman et al. (2019b)</a></cite>. In <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{top-k}\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>top</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>sampling</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{top-k}\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math>, only the <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most probable tokens are considered, and one is sampled according to its probability. In <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{nucleus}\thinspace\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>nucleus</mi><mo lspace="0.337em">⁡</mo><mi>sampling</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{nucleus}\thinspace\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model selects the smallest set of top-ranked tokens whose cumulative probability exceeds a threshold <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, and samples from this adaptive set. These techniques prevent degeneration and repetition, making them essential in modern LLM-based systems.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sampling over <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span>.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As established, our <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> algorithm is polynomial and thus compatible with HE; we confirm its correctness empirically in <a href="#S4" title="4 Empirical Evaluation ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sect.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Next, we propose efficient methods for sampling over <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span>, which build on top of our <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The first naive attempt is to use the <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inverse transform sampling</span> method, which generates random samples from a given probability distribution using its <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">cumulative distribution function (CDF)</span>: Given an encrypted <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">probability density function (PDF)</span> <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">CDF</span> <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, the method samples a plaintext value <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\sim\mathrm{Uniform}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Uniform</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\sim\mathrm{Uniform}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and returns <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=F_{X}^{-1}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=F_{X}^{-1}(u)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{X}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{X}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the inverse <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">CDF</span> (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{iCDF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>iCDF</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{iCDF}</annotation></semantics></math>), and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a sample from <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. This sampling method is inefficient as it requires at least one <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span> heavy comparison (<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Approximations for HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>) to sample an element.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Using Gumbel Distribution.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can improve upon the above by using the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gumbel}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Gumbel</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gumbel}</annotation></semantics></math> distribution. We begin by recalling the relationship between the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gumbel}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Gumbel</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gumbel}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Uniform}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Uniform</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Uniform}</annotation></semantics></math> distributions, as well as the <em id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gumbel-max trick</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gumbel (1954)</a></cite><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\sim{}\mathrm{Uniform}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Uniform</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\sim{}\mathrm{Uniform}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=-\log(-\log U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=-\log(-\log U)</annotation></semantics></math> has
a Gumbel distribution <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\sim{}\mathrm{Gumbel}(\mu=0,\beta=1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Gumbel</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\sim{}\mathrm{Gumbel}(\mu=0,\beta=1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Gumbel-Max Trick <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maddison et al. (2014)</a></cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in R^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in R^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}\sim\mathrm{Gumbel}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Gumbel</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}\sim\mathrm{Gumbel}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> be independent for each <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the random variable <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\operatorname{argmax}_{i}\left(X_{i}+G_{i}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmax</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\operatorname{argmax}_{i}\left(X_{i}+G_{i}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
follows a categorical distribution with probabilities given by,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(Y=i)=\frac{e^{X_{i}}}{\sum_{j}e^{X_{j}}}=Softmax(X_{i})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(Y=i)=\frac{e^{X_{i}}}{\sum_{j}e^{X_{j}}}=Softmax(X_{i})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using the above, our sampling method is as follows: for a given encrypted logits array <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, sample unencrypted <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathrm{Uniform}(0,1)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Uniform</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathrm{Uniform}(0,1)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and convert it to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\sim{}\mathrm{Gumbel}(0,1)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Gumbel</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\sim{}\mathrm{Gumbel}(0,1)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then compute <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}(X+G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CutMax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}(X+G)</annotation></semantics></math> over <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span>, which only requires fast additions and one call to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Efficient Nucleus Sampling.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While our Gumbel-based method allows efficient sampling over <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span>, we can do better. We aim to develop an algorithm inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maddison et al. (2014)</a></cite> that will enable us to sample only from the top of the distribution, as in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{nucleus}\thinspace\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>nucleus</mi><mo lspace="0.337em">⁡</mo><mi>sampling</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{nucleus}\thinspace\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that words from the tail of the logits distribution could not be sampled, and potentially disrupt the coherence of the generated text. To this end, we aim to sample <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> from a <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">PDF</span> that satisfy the condition <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(G&gt;p)=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(G&gt;p)=p</annotation></semantics></math>. Luckily, the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Beta</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> distribution has this property when <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\sim{}\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Beta</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\sim{}\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(G&gt;p)=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(G&gt;p)=p</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\frac{\ln(1-p)}{\ln(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\frac{\ln(1-p)}{\ln(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>  .</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">CDF</span> of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Beta</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,1)}(x)=x^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>Beta</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,1)}(x)=x^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The requirement <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(G&gt;p)=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(G&gt;p)=p</annotation></semantics></math> holds if and only if <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,1)}(p)=1-p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>Beta</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,1)}(p)=1-p</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus,
<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\alpha}=1-p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>α</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\alpha}=1-p</annotation></semantics></math>, or alternatively, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\frac{\ln(1-p)}{\ln(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\frac{\ln(1-p)}{\ln(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_observation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ Efficient Nucleus Sampling. ‣ 3.3 HE-Friendly Nucleus Sampling ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> targets <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(G&gt;p)=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(G&gt;p)=p</annotation></semantics></math>. We can generalize this result by asking <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(G&gt;p)=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(G&gt;p)=q</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, which yields <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\frac{\ln(1-q)}{\ln(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\frac{\ln(1-q)}{\ln(p)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="alg3" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 3</span> </span> Nucleus One-Shot Sampling</figcaption>
<div id="alg3.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg3.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
                
                
              Logits <math id="alg3.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\![X]\!]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\![X]\!]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg3.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>; Nucleus mass <math id="alg3.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
                
                
              Encrypted one-hot sample <math id="alg3.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\![z]\!]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>z</mi><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\![z]\!]</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
                
                
              <math id="alg3.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\frac{\ln(1-p)}{\ln(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\frac{\ln(1-p)}{\ln(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
                
                
              <math id="alg3.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
                
                
              <math id="alg3.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\leftarrow\mathrm{Uniform}(0,1)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>Uniform</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\leftarrow\mathrm{Uniform}(0,1)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
                
                
              <math id="alg3.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\left(F^{-1}_{\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,\beta)}(U_{i})\right)_{i\leq 1,\ldots,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>Beta</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\left(F^{-1}_{\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,\beta)}(U_{i})\right)_{i\leq 1,\ldots,n}</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg3.l6.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg3.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Unencrypted noise
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
                
                
              <math id="alg3.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\![\tilde{}X]\!]=[\![X]\!]\oplus G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mphantom></mphantom><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\![\tilde{}X]\!]=[\![X]\!]\oplus G</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="alg3.l7.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg3.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Perturb logits
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg3.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
                
                
              <span id="alg3.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg3.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}([\![\tilde{}X]\!])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CutMax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mphantom></mphantom><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}([\![\tilde{}X]\!])</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#alg3" title="In Efficient Nucleus Sampling. ‣ 3.3 HE-Friendly Nucleus Sampling ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> introduces a single-shot, nucleus-restricted sampling routine based on <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ Efficient Nucleus Sampling. ‣ 3.3 HE-Friendly Nucleus Sampling ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>. It starts by draw <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\sim{}\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Beta</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\sim{}\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> (Line 4). Subsequently, we form the “cut-noise” logits at Line 5, so that only those tokens whose cumulative mass exceeds <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> can potentially become the maximum. Finally, the algorithm executes a single invocation of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> at Line 6 to
homomorphically recover the one-hot encrypted sample from the nucleus set.
To summarize, we introduced a one-shot nucleus sampling algorithm that costs only one <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> when evaluated over <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Differentiability for Sequence Optimization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key property of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> and our <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{nucleus}\thinspace\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>nucleus</mi><mo lspace="0.337em">⁡</mo><mi>sampling</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{nucleus}\thinspace\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math> is their composition from polynomial operations (additions, multiplications, means/variances, <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{InvSqr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>InvSqr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{InvSqr}</annotation></semantics></math>, and elementwise powers). In the plaintext domain (Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.1 The CutMax Algorithm ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), these operations are naturally differentiable without any approximations: for example, the <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{inverse-square-root}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>inverse</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>square</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>root</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{inverse-square-root}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> smooth and differentiable for <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling exact gradient computation via automatic differentiation. This contrasts with prior tournament/league methods, which inherently rely on the discontinuous SIGN function—even in plaintext, SIGN requires smoothing or approximation for differentiability, often leading to high-degree polynomials that can introduce gradient instabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In HE, both approaches use polynomial approximations, but CutMax approximates smooth functions like <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be achieved with lower-degree polynomials compared to approximating the sharp discontinuity of SIGN (as evidenced by the lower multiplicative depths in Table <a href="#A2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Multiplication Depth. ‣ B.1 Goldschmidt ‣ Appendix B Approximations for HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). Theoretically, polynomial composition preserves differentiability, providing a rigorous foundation for exact end-to-end gradients through the full forward pass.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This stands in contrast to non-differentiable ops like discrete <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>sampling</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math>, which require approximations like straight-through estimators (STE; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bengio et al. (2013)</a></cite>) during backpropagation—where gradients are passed ‘straight through’ the hard <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> as if it were a soft identity. STE introduces bias and can lead to optimization instabilities in sequence tasks (e.g., reinforcement learning from human feedback or sequence fine-tuning).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Theoretically, our methods serve as drop-in polynomial surrogates: during training, replace hard decoding with <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> (for greedy) or <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{nucleus}\thinspace\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>nucleus</mi><mo lspace="0.337em">⁡</mo><mi>sampling</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{nucleus}\thinspace\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math> based upon <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> (for <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{sampling}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>sampling</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{sampling}</annotation></semantics></math>), and compute exact gradients end-to-end. For instance, in a sequence loss <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{y},\hat{\mathbf{y}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{y},\hat{\mathbf{y}})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated via decoding, the gradient <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathcal{L}/\partial\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathcal{L}/\partial\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> (w.r.t. input logits <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>) flows precisely through the polynomial chain. While we leave empirical validation (e.g., on BLEU/ROUGE improvements in fine-tuning) to future work, the theoretical differentiability—rooted in smooth plaintext operations and polynomial preservation—positions <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> as a promising alternative for stable, gradient-based sequence optimization.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Empirical Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate our methods, we use the <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ccdv/arxiv-summarization</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohan et al. (2018)</a></cite> dataset of paper abstracts, sampling <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=100</annotation></semantics></math> articles unless otherwise specified. We report results on realistic LLM logits from models including QWEN2.5-0.5B (<math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}|\approx 150\mathrm{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>150</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}|\approx 150\mathrm{K}</annotation></semantics></math>), Mistral-7B (<math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}|\approx 33\mathrm{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>33</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}|\approx 33\mathrm{K}</annotation></semantics></math>), and GPT-2 (<math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}|\approx 50\mathrm{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}|\approx 50\mathrm{K}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Plain-Text CutMax</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> in the unencrypted domain on the QWEN2.5-0.5B model (vocabulary size <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}|\approx 150\mathrm{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>150</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}|\approx 150\mathrm{K}</annotation></semantics></math>), using the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ccdv/arxiv-summarization</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohan et al. (2018)</a></cite> dataset of paper abstracts. We sampled <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=100</annotation></semantics></math> articles and, for each, retrieved the next-token logits via one-step greedy decoding (<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">greedy argmax</span> on the plaintext logits), yielding 100 ground-truth tokens. For each prompt we then ran <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\{2,3,4,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\{2,3,4,5\}</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, grid-searching over odd powers <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\{7,9,\dots,19\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>19</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\{7,9,\dots,19\}</annotation></semantics></math> and scaling factors <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\{3,5,\dots,39\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>39</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\{3,5,\dots,39\}</annotation></semantics></math> to find the smallest <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,c)</annotation></semantics></math></em> that still achieves perfect next-token recovery.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Convergence vs. #iterations.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.T1" title="In Convergence vs. #iterations. ‣ 4.1 Plain-Text CutMax ‣ 4 Empirical Evaluation ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes, for each <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, the best <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,c)</annotation></semantics></math> choice, the resulting average maximum normalized score after <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> iterations,
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Argmax}_{i}=Z_{i}/\sum_{j=1}^{\mathcal{V}}Z_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Argmax</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Argmax}_{i}=Z_{i}/\sum_{j=1}^{\mathcal{V}}Z_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the recovery rate (fraction of times <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Argmax}_{i}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Argmax</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Argmax}_{i}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> matches the true token). The results show rapid convergence: even with just <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=2</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> concentrates <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 95\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the mass on the correct token. By <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=4</annotation></semantics></math>, the recovery exceeds <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99.99\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>99.99</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99.99\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=5</annotation></semantics></math>, it achieves exact recovery in all cases.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Convergence of <math id="S4.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> on QWEN2.5-0.5B (<math id="S4.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}|\approx 150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>150</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}|\approx 150</annotation></semantics></math>K) over 100 arXiv abstracts. We report the mean of the per-prompt <math id="S4.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Argmax}_{i}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Argmax</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Argmax}_{i}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, the best (i.e. largest) <math id="S4.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Argmax}_{i}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Argmax</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Argmax}_{i}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> observed across the 100 prompts with exact next-token recovery of 100% in all cases.</figcaption>
