<span class="ltx_bibblock"><a href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/991" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">MOAI: Module-optimizing architecture for non-interactive secure transformer inference</a>.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">Cryptology ePrint Archive, Paper 2025/991.

</span></li>
      
<li id="bib.bib39" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Zhang et al. (2024b)</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
Peng Zhang, Ao Duan, and Hengrui Lu. 2024b.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/cryptography8020018" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">An efficient homomorphic argmax approximation for privacy-preserving neural networks</a>.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><em id="bib.bib39.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cryptography</em>, 8(2).

</span></li>
      
<li id="bib.bib40" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Zimerman et al. (2024a)</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
Itamar Zimerman, Allon Adir, Ehud Aharoni, Matan Avitan, Moran Baruch, Nir Drucker, Jenny Lerner, Ramy Masalha, Reut Meiri, and Omri Soceanu. 2024a.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.09457" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Power-softmax: Towards secure llm inference over encrypted data</a>.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><em id="bib.bib40.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.09457</em>.

</span></li>
      
<li id="bib.bib41" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Zimerman et al. (2024b)</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
Itamar Zimerman, Moran Baruch, Nir Drucker, Gilad Ezov, Omri Soceanu, and Lior Wolf. 2024b.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock"><a href="https://doi.org/10.5555/3692070.3694670" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Converting transformers to polynomial form for secure inference over homomorphic encryption</a>.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">In <em id="bib.bib41.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Proceedings of the 41st International Conference on Machine Learning</em>, ICML’24. JMLR.org.

</span></li>
    
</ul>
</section>
<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix</h2>

</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Threat model</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are two commonly used threat-models for secure inference and generative AI for LLMs over HE: 1) using encrypted weights; or 2) using encrypted input samples (queries). When considering a 2-party scenario with <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span>, it is commonly assumed that one party is the (untrusted, semi-honest) server who holds the model (encrypted or not) and the other party is the data-owner who performs the query and would like to avoid revealing the query to the server. The decision of whether the server holds the model encrypted or not depends on whether a third-party the model-owner agrees to share the data with the server. Our proposed solution is orthogonal to the above decision, which eventually only affects latency. All communications are performed using TLS 1.3 and privacy attacks such as model extraction attack and membership inference attacks require extra mechanism such as differential privacy, which is also orthogonal to this work. For brevity we have decided to refer the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Adir et al. (2024)</a></cite>[Chapter 3] that further describes this security model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Approximations for <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span></h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since HE supports only polynomial operations, functions like <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>max</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span>, <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Inv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Inv</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Inv}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{InvSqr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>InvSqr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{InvSqr}</annotation></semantics></math> must be approximated. A common formulation for <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="A2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(a,b)=\dfrac{a+b}{2}+\dfrac{\textsc{sign}(a-b)\cdot(a-b)}{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(a,b)=\dfrac{a+b}{2}+\dfrac{\textsc{sign}(a-b)\cdot(a-b)}{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{sign}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{sign}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise. An approximation of <span id="A2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> is given, e.g., in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moon et al. (2024a)</a></cite>. To make the results of <span id="A2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> suitable as an indicator function (as in <a href="#A2.E5" title="In Appendix B Approximations for HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), it is common to translate the <span id="A2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> range from <math id="A2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{-1,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{-1,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="A2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1+\textsc{sign}(x)}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1+\textsc{sign}(x)}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <math id="A2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>max</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="A2.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> functions are used by prior-art <math id="A2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> implementations. In contrast, <math id="A2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> uses <math id="A2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Inv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Inv</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Inv}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{InvSqr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>InvSqr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{InvSqr}</annotation></semantics></math> approximations.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Goldschmidt</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Goldschmidt</span> inverse algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldschmidt (1964)</a></cite> is an iterative process for computing the function <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=\frac{1}{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=\frac{1}{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheon et al. (2019)</a></cite> showed that this algorithm can be used to approximate inputs in the range <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,2]</annotation></semantics></math> effectively using the equation</p>
<table id="A2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=\prod_{i=0}^{d}\left(1+(1-x)^{2^{i}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=\prod_{i=0}^{d}\left(1+(1-x)^{2^{i}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Specifically, they proved that for <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x\in\left[\frac{1}{2^{n}},1\right])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x\in\left[\frac{1}{2^{n}},1\right])</annotation></semantics></math>, it requires <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d=\Theta(\log\alpha+n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d=\Theta(\log\alpha+n))</annotation></semantics></math> iterations to converge, with an error bound of <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2^{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>α</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2^{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panda (2021)</a></cite> showed an efficient implementation for the Goldschmidt inverse square root (<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{InvSqr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>InvSqr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{InvSqr}</annotation></semantics></math>) algorithm over <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span>. Subsequently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moon et al. (2024a)</a></cite> reported improved measurements of the inverse and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{InvSqr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>InvSqr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{InvSqr}</annotation></semantics></math> implementations under CKKS. <a href="#A2.T3" title="In Multiplication Depth. ‣ B.1 Goldschmidt ‣ Appendix B Approximations for HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> repeats some benchmarks for the <span id="A2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{InvSqr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>InvSqr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{InvSqr}</annotation></semantics></math> functions at different approximation levels (<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>). One immediate conclusion is that the <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{InvSqr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>InvSqr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{InvSqr}</annotation></semantics></math> implementation is <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(1.5-2.1\times)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2.1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1.5-2.1\times)</annotation></semantics></math> faster compared to the <span id="A2.SS1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> implementation for the same level of errors. One reason is that <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{InvSqr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>InvSqr</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{InvSqr}</annotation></semantics></math> has a lower <span id="A2.SS1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multiplication depth</span> (defined below) and thus requires fewer bootstraps.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multiplication Depth.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In CKKS, a ciphertext must be refreshed via a costly <span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bootstrap</span> operation after every <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> sequential multiplications, where <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a scheme parameter. Since bootstrapping is significantly slower than other operations, a key optimization goal is to minimize its use. The longest chain of multiplications in a function—its <span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multiplication depth</span>, is therefore a critical target for reduction.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>A sample benchmarks of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moon et al. (2024a)</a></cite> conducted on AMD EPYC 7502-32 CPU, using Microsoft Seal, poly degree of <math id="A2.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{17}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>17</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{17}</annotation></semantics></math>, and scale <math id="A2.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=2^{40}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>40</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=2^{40}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
<table id="A2.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A2.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="A2.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm</span></td>
<td id="A2.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">7</td>
<td id="A2.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">9</td>
<td id="A2.T3.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">11</td>
<td id="A2.T3.2.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">13</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" rowspan="3"><span id="A2.T3.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span></td>
<td id="A2.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Iterations</td>
<td id="A2.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">7</td>
<td id="A2.T3.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">9</td>
<td id="A2.T3.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">11</td>
<td id="A2.T3.2.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">12</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r">Multiplication depth</td>
<td id="A2.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">14</td>
