<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Previous, Comparison-Based Methods for argmax over HE</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A3.F5" title="In Appendix C Previous, Comparison-Based Methods for argmax over HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> visualizes the two canonical comparison-based approaches for encrypted <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math>: the <em id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tournament</em> method (<a href="#A3.F5.sf1" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Appendix C Previous, Comparison-Based Methods for argmax over HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a>) and the <em id="A3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">league</em> method (<a href="#A3.F5.sf2" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Appendix C Previous, Comparison-Based Methods for argmax over HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(b)</span></a>). Both rely on repeated encrypted comparisons implemented via polynomial approximations of <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{sign}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{sign}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the tournament design, approximately <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{-}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{-}1</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise comparisons are arranged into <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{2}n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{2}n</annotation></semantics></math> sequential stages. At each stage, the number of candidates is halved until only the maximum remains. While asymptotically efficient, this design requires <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log n</annotation></semantics></math> sequential <span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> evaluations, each incurring costly rotations and bootstraps.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the league design, every pair of inputs is compared, and results are accumulated so that the true maximum is the only element with score <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{-}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{-}1</annotation></semantics></math>. This requires <math id="A3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> sequential rounds of comparisons. Although SIMD parallelism allows all <math id="A3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise comparisons within a round to be executed in parallel slots, the <em id="A3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sequential</em> round structure still dominates, leaving the method quadratic overall.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As summarized in <a href="#A2.T3" title="In Multiplication Depth. ‣ B.1 Goldschmidt ‣ Appendix B Approximations for HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, polynomial <span id="A3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> approximations are substantially deeper and slower than inverse-square-root or division. Thus, even when accelerated with SIMD, both the tournament and league methods remain inefficient at large <math id="A3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, this has limited published HE <math id="A3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> implementations either to small input sizes or to systems that decrypt logits before <math id="A3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A3.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.08383v1/imgs/argmax_tournament.png" id="A3.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/251;" width="452" height="251" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Tournament method.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A3.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.08383v1/imgs/argmax_table.png" id="A3.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/288;" width="452" height="288" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>League method.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="A3.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Illustration of Prior Comparison-based argmax Methods in HE</span> (see main text <a href="#S2" title="2 Background: Homomorphic Argmax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sect.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Argmax using SIMD</h2>

<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SIMD and Rotations.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CKKS enables SIMD-style packing, where one ciphertext encrypts a vector <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{1},\ldots,v_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{1},\ldots,v_{s})</annotation></semantics></math>. Operations are applied elementwise, and encrypted vectors can be rotated by <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&lt;s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&lt;s</annotation></semantics></math> positions. This allows for patterns such as <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{RotAndSum}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>RotAndSum</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{RotAndSum}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Adir et al. (2024)</a></cite> - which aggregates values with <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log s</annotation></semantics></math> rotations and additions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2509.08383v1/imgs/argmax_simd_full.png" id="A4.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:329/286;" width="329" height="286" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span><math id="A4.F6.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;s</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><img src="2509.08383v1/imgs/argmax_simd_partial.png" id="A4.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:329/286;" width="329" height="286" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span><math id="A4.F6.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=s</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">HE</span> <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">SIMD</span> example implementation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A4.F6" title="In SIMD and Rotations. ‣ Appendix D Argmax using SIMD ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> demonstrates how to leverage <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">SIMD</span> to execute <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> operations in parallel. Step (1) shows the encrypted input; in Step (2), we compute an indicator vector where <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M[i]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M[i]=1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A[i]&gt;A[i+\frac{n}{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A[i]&gt;A[i+\frac{n}{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>, and 0 otherwise. Subsequently, Step (3) selects (masks) only the elements that match the indicator vector, which returns the desired results.
Consider two cases: <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;s</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#A4.F6.sf1" title="In Figure 6 ‣ SIMD and Rotations. ‣ Appendix D Argmax using SIMD ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(a)</span></a>) and <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=s</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#A4.F6.sf2" title="In Figure 6 ‣ SIMD and Rotations. ‣ Appendix D Argmax using SIMD ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(b)</span></a>) (The case <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&lt;s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&lt;s</annotation></semantics></math> behaves similarly to <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=s</annotation></semantics></math>). Here, <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximal number of elements that a single ciphertext can encrypt. <a href="#A4.F6.sf1" title="In Figure 6 ‣ SIMD and Rotations. ‣ Appendix D Argmax using SIMD ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(a)</span></a> illustrates the case where <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=16</annotation></semantics></math> elements are encrypted in two ciphertexts, each containing a vector of 8 elements. In contrast, <a href="#A4.F6.sf2" title="In Figure 6 ‣ SIMD and Rotations. ‣ Appendix D Argmax using SIMD ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(b)</span></a> shows the case <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=s</annotation></semantics></math>, which requires only one ciphertext. In the first case, no rotation is needed, as we can directly subtract one ciphertext from the other using element-wise subtraction. However, this is not the case in <a href="#A4.F6.sf2" title="In Figure 6 ‣ SIMD and Rotations. ‣ Appendix D Argmax using SIMD ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(b)</span></a>, where we must homomorphically rotate <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/2</annotation></semantics></math> positions. As a result, the output is duplicated, and we only need to keep the top <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/2</annotation></semantics></math> elements.
It appears that many comparisons can be saved by using <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref">SIMD</span>. Consider the case where <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=s</annotation></semantics></math> for the league method (<a href="#A3.F5.sf2" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Appendix C Previous, Comparison-Based Methods for argmax over HE ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(b)</span></a>) that requires only <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> homomorphic rotations and <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> parallel <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> evaluations, with one additional final parallel <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> evaluation. The complexity is thus reduced from <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_smallcaps">sign</span> operations, which, on hardware with sufficient parallel threads, may result in faster execution than the sequential tournament algorithm. When <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;s</annotation></semantics></math> it is worth combining the methods as was done in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iliashenko and Zucca (2021)</a></cite>. Starting with a tournament and when the number of elements is reduced beyond some threshold continue with the league method.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Convergence of <math id="A5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section proves convergence of <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#alg1" title="In 3.1 The CutMax Algorithm ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) in the notation of the main text.
Throughout, fix <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, an odd integer <math id="A5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, and a scaling constant <math id="A5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="A5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{Y}^{(t)}=({Y}^{(t)}_{1},\ldots,{Y}^{(t)}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{Y}^{(t)}=({Y}^{(t)}_{1},\ldots,{Y}^{(t)}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the state at iteration <math id="A5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>,
with mean and variance</p>
<table id="A5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{(t)}\;:=\;\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}{Y}^{(t)}_{i},\qquad(s^{(t)})^{2}\;:=\;\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Bigl({Y}^{(t)}_{i}-\mu^{(t)}\Bigr)^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{(t)}\;:=\;\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}{Y}^{(t)}_{i},\qquad(s^{(t)})^{2}\;:=\;\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Bigl({Y}^{(t)}_{i}-\mu^{(t)}\Bigr)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and standardized residuals</p>
<table id="A5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{(t)}_{i}\;:=\;\frac{{Y}^{(t)}_{i}-\mu^{(t)}}{s^{(t)}}\,,\qquad i\in[n]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{(t)}_{i}\;:=\;\frac{{Y}^{(t)}_{i}-\mu^{(t)}}{s^{(t)}}\,,\qquad i\in[n].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">One <math id="A5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> inner update (Lines 3–6 of <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.1 The CutMax Algorithm ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is</p>
<table id="A5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{(t)}_{i}\;:=\;1+\frac{r^{(t)}_{i}}{c},\qquad Y^{(t+1)}_{i}\;:=\;\Bigl(Z^{(t)}_{i}\Bigr)^{p}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{(t)}_{i}\;:=\;1+\frac{r^{(t)}_{i}}{c},\qquad Y^{(t+1)}_{i}\;:=\;\Bigl(Z^{(t)}_{i}\Bigr)^{p},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p1.4" class="ltx_p">and the final normalization (Line 7) forms</p>
<table id="A5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}\;=\;\frac{Y^{(T)}_{i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{n}Y^{(T)}_{j}}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}\;=\;\frac{Y^{(T)}_{i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{n}Y^{(T)}_{j}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\star}:=\operatorname{argmax}_{i}Y^{(1)}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmax</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\star}:=\operatorname{argmax}_{i}Y^{(1)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the unique maximizer index of the input (ties can be broken arbitrarily).
The proof proceeds in four steps.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step 1: standardization identities and sharp bounds.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For every <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A5.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n}r^{(t)}_{i}=0,\qquad\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\bigl(r^{(t)}_{i}\bigr)^{2}=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n}r^{(t)}_{i}=0,\qquad\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\bigl(r^{(t)}_{i}\bigr)^{2}=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Moreover, the following uniform <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> bound holds:</p>
<table id="A5.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{i\in[n]}\bigl|r^{(t)}_{i}\bigr|\;\leq\;\sqrt{\,n-1\,}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{i\in[n]}\bigl|r^{(t)}_{i}\bigr|\;\leq\;\sqrt{\,n-1\,}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><em id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Justification.</em>
The identities in (<a href="#A5.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Step 1: standardization identities and sharp bounds. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) follow from the definitions of <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For (<a href="#A5.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Step 1: standardization identities and sharp bounds. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), fix <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and note
<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\neq k}r^{(t)}_{i}=-r^{(t)}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\neq k}r^{(t)}_{i}=-r^{(t)}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> by (<a href="#A5.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Step 1: standardization identities and sharp bounds. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
By Cauchy–Schwarz,
<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|r^{(t)}_{k}|=\bigl|\sum_{i\neq k}r^{(t)}_{i}\bigr|\leq\sqrt{n-1}\cdot\sqrt{\sum_{i\neq k}(r^{(t)}_{i})^{2}}=\sqrt{n-1}\cdot\sqrt{n-(r^{(t)}_{k})^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|r^{(t)}_{k}|=\bigl|\sum_{i\neq k}r^{(t)}_{i}\bigr|\leq\sqrt{n-1}\cdot\sqrt{\sum_{i\neq k}(r^{(t)}_{i})^{2}}=\sqrt{n-1}\cdot\sqrt{n-(r^{(t)}_{k})^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which implies <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|r^{(t)}_{k}|\leq\sqrt{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|r^{(t)}_{k}|\leq\sqrt{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step 2: positivity and order preservation.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume</p>
<table id="A5.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\;&gt;\;\sqrt{\,n-1\,}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">&gt;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\;&gt;\;\sqrt{\,n-1\,}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Then for every <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and every <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{(t)}_{i}\;=\;1+\frac{r^{(t)}_{i}}{c}\;\in\;\Bigl(1-\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c},\,1+\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\Bigr)\;\subset\;(0,2)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{(t)}_{i}\;=\;1+\frac{r^{(t)}_{i}}{c}\;\in\;\Bigl(1-\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c},\,1+\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\Bigr)\;\subset\;(0,2),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">by (<a href="#A5.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Step 1: standardization identities and sharp bounds. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).
Hence <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{(t)}_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{(t)}_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{(t+1)}_{i}=(Z^{(t)}_{i})^{p}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{(t+1)}_{i}=(Z^{(t)}_{i})^{p}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The maps <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\mapsto(x-\mu^{(t)})/s^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\mapsto(x-\mu^{(t)})/s^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\mapsto 1+u/c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\mapsto 1+u/c</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\mapsto v^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\mapsto v^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> (odd <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) are strictly increasing, and normalization by a positive scalar preserves order.
Therefore,</p>
<table id="A5.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{argmax}_{i}Y^{(t)}_{i}\;=\;\operatorname{argmax}_{i}Y^{(t+1)}_{i}\;=\;i^{\star}\qquad\text{for all }t\geq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>argmax</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmax</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{argmax}_{i}Y^{(t)}_{i}\;=\;\operatorname{argmax}_{i}Y^{(t+1)}_{i}\;=\;i^{\star}\qquad\text{for all }t\geq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step 3: two-level manifold and its image in closed form.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Residuals are invariant under positive scaling:
for any <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\alpha{Y}^{(t)}_{i}-\alpha\mu^{(t)}}{\alpha s^{(t)}}\;=\;r^{(t)}_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\alpha{Y}^{(t)}_{i}-\alpha\mu^{(t)}}{\alpha s^{(t)}}\;=\;r^{(t)}_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Define the normalized state <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Y}^{(t)}:=Y^{(t)}/\sum_{j}Y^{(t)}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Y}^{(t)}:=Y^{(t)}/\sum_{j}Y^{(t)}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (so <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}\widehat{Y}^{(t)}_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}\widehat{Y}^{(t)}_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>).
Let</p>
<table id="A5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}\;:=\;\Bigl\{\,y\in(0,1)^{n}:\ \sum_{i}y_{i}=1,\ y_{i^{\star}}=s,\ y_{j}=\tfrac{1-s}{n-1}\ \forall j\neq i^{\star},\ s\in(1/n,1)\Bigr\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.611em">:</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.667em" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\;:=\;\Bigl\{\,y\in(0,1)^{n}:\ \sum_{i}y_{i}=1,\ y_{i^{\star}}=s,\ y_{j}=\tfrac{1-s}{n-1}\ \forall j\neq i^{\star},\ s\in(1/n,1)\Bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">be the two-level manifold (top coordinate <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, non-top coordinates equal).
On <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> the standardized residuals are <em id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">explicit</em> and independent of <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A5.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r_{i^{\star}}=\sqrt{n-1},\qquad r_{j}=-\frac{1}{\sqrt{n-1}}\ \ (j\neq i^{\star})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i^{\star}}=\sqrt{n-1},\qquad r_{j}=-\frac{1}{\sqrt{n-1}}\ \ (j\neq i^{\star}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">as the system <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+(n-1)b=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+(n-1)b=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{2}+(n-1)b^{2}=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{2}+(n-1)b^{2}=n</annotation></semantics></math> has the unique solution
<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\sqrt{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\sqrt{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="b=-1/\sqrt{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=-1/\sqrt{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, for any <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{i^{\star}}=1+\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c},\qquad Z_{j}=1-\frac{1}{c\sqrt{\,n-1\,}}\ \ (j\neq i^{\star})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i^{\star}}=1+\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c},\qquad Z_{j}=1-\frac{1}{c\sqrt{\,n-1\,}}\ \ (j\neq i^{\star}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">and after the power and normalization (one <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> inner update),
the image is the <em id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">same</em> vector for all <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A5.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{+}\;=\;\Bigl(s^{\star},\ \underbrace{\tfrac{1-s^{\star}}{n-1},\ldots,\tfrac{1-s^{\star}}{n-1}}_{n-1\text{ times}}\Bigr),\qquad s^{\star}\;=\;\frac{\Bigl(1+\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\Bigr)^{p}}{\Bigl(1+\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\Bigr)^{p}+(n-1)\Bigl(1-\frac{1}{c\sqrt{n-1}}\Bigr)^{p}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> times</mtext></mrow></mrow></munder><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{+}\;=\;\Bigl(s^{\star},\ \underbrace{\tfrac{1-s^{\star}}{n-1},\ldots,\tfrac{1-s^{\star}}{n-1}}_{n-1\text{ times}}\Bigr),\qquad s^{\star}\;=\;\frac{\Bigl(1+\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\Bigr)^{p}}{\Bigl(1+\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\Bigr)^{p}+(n-1)\Bigl(1-\frac{1}{c\sqrt{n-1}}\Bigr)^{p}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.6" class="ltx_p">Consequently, <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped in one step to the <em id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single</em> two-level vector (<a href="#A5.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Step 3: two-level manifold and its image in closed form. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step 4: global convergence to the two–level fixed point.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show that any trajectory with a unique maximizer converges to the two–level vector in (<a href="#A5.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Step 3: two-level manifold and its image in closed form. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Set–up and notation.</span>
Work with the normalized state <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}y_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}y_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>); this does not affect residuals or the identity of the maximizer.
Let <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> denote “one <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> inner update (Lines 3–6) followed by normalization”.
Fix the (unique) maximizer index <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\star}=\operatorname{argmax}_{i}y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmax</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\star}=\operatorname{argmax}_{i}y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Write residuals <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a:=r_{i^{\star}}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a:=r_{i^{\star}}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}:=r_{j}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}:=r_{j}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq i^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq i^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>; then by (<a href="#A5.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Step 1: standardization identities and sharp bounds. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>),
<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}:=\frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}b_{j}=-\frac{a}{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}:=\frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}b_{j}=-\frac{a}{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and all residuals lie in
<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-\sqrt{n-1},\,\sqrt{n-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-\sqrt{n-1},\,\sqrt{n-1}]</annotation></semantics></math> by (<a href="#A5.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Step 1: standardization identities and sharp bounds. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).
Let</p>
<table id="A5.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(u):=\Bigl(1+\frac{u}{c}\Bigr)^{p},\qquad g_{j}:=\phi(b_{j}),\quad A:=\phi(a),\quad S:=\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}g_{j},\quad D:=A+S." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></munder><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(u):=\Bigl(1+\frac{u}{c}\Bigr)^{p},\qquad g_{j}:=\phi(b_{j}),\quad A:=\phi(a),\quad S:=\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}g_{j},\quad D:=A+S.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">One <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> step and normalization yield</p>
<table id="A5.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F(y)_{i^{\star}}=\frac{A}{D},\qquad F(y)_{j}=\frac{g_{j}}{D}\ \ (j\neq i^{\star})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>A</mi><mi>D</mi></mfrac><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>D</mi></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(y)_{i^{\star}}=\frac{A}{D},\qquad F(y)_{j}=\frac{g_{j}}{D}\ \ (j\neq i^{\star}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A Lyapunov functional on residuals.</span>
Measure the dispersion of the non–tops in <em id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">residual</em> space by</p>
<table id="A5.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(y)\;:=\;\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}\bigl(b_{j}-\overline{b}\bigr)^{2}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(y)\;:=\;\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}\bigl(b_{j}-\overline{b}\bigr)^{2}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(y)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(y)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, with equality iff the non–top residuals are all equal (equivalently, <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bounding the non–top deviations after one step.</span>
By the mean value theorem, for each <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq i^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq i^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> there exists <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{b}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="A5.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{j}-\overline{g}\;=\;\phi(b_{j})-\phi(\overline{b})\;=\;\phi^{\prime}(\xi_{j})\,(b_{j}-\overline{b}),\qquad\overline{g}:=\frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}g_{j}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></munder><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{j}-\overline{g}\;=\;\phi(b_{j})-\phi(\overline{b})\;=\;\phi^{\prime}(\xi_{j})\,(b_{j}-\overline{b}),\qquad\overline{g}:=\frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}g_{j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in[-\sqrt{n-1},\sqrt{n-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in[-\sqrt{n-1},\sqrt{n-1}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;\sqrt{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;\sqrt{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> (assumption (<a href="#A5.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Step 2: positivity and order preservation. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>)),</p>
<table id="A5.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}(u)\;=\;\frac{p}{c}\Bigl(1+\frac{u}{c}\Bigr)^{p-1}\in\Bigl[m,\,M\Bigr],\quad m:=\frac{p}{c}\Bigl(1-\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\Bigr)^{p-1},\quad M:=\frac{p}{c}\Bigl(1+\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\Bigr)^{p-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>u</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}(u)\;=\;\frac{p}{c}\Bigl(1+\frac{u}{c}\Bigr)^{p-1}\in\Bigl[m,\,M\Bigr],\quad m:=\frac{p}{c}\Bigl(1-\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\Bigr)^{p-1},\quad M:=\frac{p}{c}\Bigl(1+\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\Bigr)^{p-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Hence</p>
<table id="A5.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}\!(g_{j}-\overline{g})^{2}\;=\;\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}\!\bigl(\phi^{\prime}(\xi_{j})\bigr)^{2}\,(b_{j}-\overline{b})^{2}\;\leq\;M^{2}\,\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}\!(b_{j}-\overline{b})^{2}\;=\;M^{2}\,\mathcal{U}(y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}\!(g_{j}-\overline{g})^{2}\;=\;\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}\!\bigl(\phi^{\prime}(\xi_{j})\bigr)^{2}\,(b_{j}-\overline{b})^{2}\;\leq\;M^{2}\,\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}\!(b_{j}-\overline{b})^{2}\;=\;M^{2}\,\mathcal{U}(y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.4" class="ltx_p">After normalization by the positive scalar <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, the non–top deviations from their mean scale by <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/D</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A5.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}\!\Bigl(F(y)_{j}-\frac{1-F(y)_{i^{\star}}}{n-1}\Bigr)^{2}\;=\;\frac{1}{D^{2}}\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}\!(g_{j}-\overline{g})^{2}\;\leq\;\frac{M^{2}}{D^{2}}\,\mathcal{U}(y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}\!\Bigl(F(y)_{j}-\frac{1-F(y)_{i^{\star}}}{n-1}\Bigr)^{2}\;=\;\frac{1}{D^{2}}\sum_{j\neq i^{\star}}\!(g_{j}-\overline{g})^{2}\;\leq\;\frac{M^{2}}{D^{2}}\,\mathcal{U}(y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.5" class="ltx_p">Thus</p>
<table id="A5.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}\bigl(F(y)\bigr)\ \leq\ \frac{M^{2}}{D^{2}}\,\mathcal{U}(y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}\bigl(F(y)\bigr)\ \leq\ \frac{M^{2}}{D^{2}}\,\mathcal{U}(y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A uniform lower bound on the normalizer.</span>
For every coordinate <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in[-\sqrt{n-1},\sqrt{n-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in[-\sqrt{n-1},\sqrt{n-1}]</annotation></semantics></math> we have
<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(u)\geq\bigl(1-\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\bigr)^{\!p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(u)\geq\bigl(1-\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\bigr)^{\!p}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence</p>
<table id="A5.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\;=\;A+S\;\geq\;n\Bigl(1-\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\Bigr)^{\!p}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\;=\;A+S\;\geq\;n\Bigl(1-\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\Bigr)^{\!p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Combining this with <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, (<a href="#A5.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ Step 4: global convergence to the two–level fixed point. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) gives the explicit contraction</p>
<table id="A5.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}\!\bigl(F(y)\bigr)\ \leq\ \kappa\,\mathcal{U}(y),\qquad\kappa:=\frac{\bigl(\tfrac{p}{c}\bigr)^{2}\bigl(1+\tfrac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\bigr)^{2(p-1)}}{\,n^{2}\bigl(1-\tfrac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\bigr)^{2p}}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.555em;" width="0.555em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}\!\bigl(F(y)\bigr)\ \leq\ \kappa\,\mathcal{U}(y),\qquad\kappa:=\frac{\bigl(\tfrac{p}{c}\bigr)^{2}\bigl(1+\tfrac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\bigr)^{2(p-1)}}{\,n^{2}\bigl(1-\tfrac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\bigr)^{2p}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For the <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,p,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,p,c)</annotation></semantics></math> used in practice we have <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>; in particular, <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> holds whenever <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is not arbitrarily close to <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> (for fixed <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,p</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem E.1</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(CutMax convergence)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;\sqrt{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;\sqrt{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the input has a unique maximizer <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the normalized <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{CutMax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CutMax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{CutMax}</annotation></semantics></math> iterates converge to the two–level vector in
(<a href="#A5.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Step 3: two-level manifold and its image in closed form. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). Moreover, the non–top residual dispersion
satisfies the geometric contraction</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}\bigl(\widehat{Y}^{(t+1)}\bigr)\ \leq\ \kappa\,\mathcal{U}\bigl(\widehat{Y}^{(t)}\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}\bigl(\widehat{Y}^{(t+1)}\bigr)\ \leq\ \kappa\,\mathcal{U}\bigl(\widehat{Y}^{(t)}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> given in (<a href="#A5.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Step 4: global convergence to the two–level fixed point. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Order preservation is Step 2; the two–level image is Step 3. In Step 4 we
proved
<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(F(y))\leq\frac{M^{2}}{D^{2}}\,\mathcal{U}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(F(y))\leq\frac{M^{2}}{D^{2}}\,\mathcal{U}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\frac{p}{c}\bigl(1+\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\bigr)^{p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\frac{p}{c}\bigl(1+\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\bigr)^{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\geq n\bigl(1-\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\bigr)^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>c</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\geq n\bigl(1-\frac{\sqrt{n-1}}{c}\bigr)^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, which yields
<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(\widehat{Y}^{(t+1)})\leq\kappa\,\mathcal{U}(\widehat{Y}^{(t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(\widehat{Y}^{(t+1)})\leq\kappa\,\mathcal{U}(\widehat{Y}^{(t)})</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#A5.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Step 4: global convergence to the two–level fixed point. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>). Hence
<math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(\widehat{Y}^{(t)})\!\to\!0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(\widehat{Y}^{(t)})\!\to\!0</annotation></semantics></math>, so the iterates approach the
two–level manifold, and by Step 3 the very next update maps any
two–level point to (<a href="#A5.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Step 3: two-level manifold and its image in closed form. ‣ Appendix E Convergence of CutMax ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Anecdotal Sampling Demonstration</h2>

<figure id="A6.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08383v1/sampling_iteration_.svg" id="A6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:238/537;" width="238" height="537"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span><span id="A6.F7.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison of Sampling Methods on GPT-2 Last-Token Decoding:</span>
The top strip shows the complete 50,257<span id="A6.F7.14" class="ltx_text">-</span>dimensional logit vector (only its outline is visible at this scale), and the second strip zooms in on the five largest logits. The three lower strips plot, for 1,000 repeated draws, histograms of the 30 most frequently chosen token indices produced by four different samplers: standard <math id="A6.F7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Softmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Softmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Softmax}</annotation></semantics></math> at temperature 1, the classical Gumbel<span id="A6.F7.15" class="ltx_text">-</span>Max trick, inverse<span id="A6.F7.16" class="ltx_text">-</span>transform sampling on the normalized exponentials (“Exp<span id="A6.F7.17" class="ltx_text">-</span>inverse”), and our one<span id="A6.F7.18" class="ltx_text">-</span>shot <em id="A6.F7.19" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Beta<span id="A6.F7.19.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>cut nucleus</em> sampler, which perturbs each logit with i.i.d. <math id="A6.F7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}\!\sim\!\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∼</mo><mrow><mi>Beta</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}\!\sim\!\mathrm{Beta}(\alpha,1)</annotation></semantics></math> noise using <math id="A6.F7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.486" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.486</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.486</annotation></semantics></math> computed from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Observation 3.4. ‣ Efficient Nucleus Sampling. ‣ 3.3 HE-Friendly Nucleus Sampling ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Observation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> for <math id="A6.F7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)=(0.9,0.95)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.95</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)=(0.9,0.95)</annotation></semantics></math>. Whereas the first three methods still allocate non<span id="A6.F7.20" class="ltx_text">-</span>negligible mass to tail tokens, the Beta<span id="A6.F7.21" class="ltx_text">-</span>cut histogram is sharply truncated, confirming that all probability remains inside the top<span id="A6.F7.22" class="ltx_text">-</span><math id="A6.F7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> nucleus exactly as required by the HE<span id="A6.F7.23" class="ltx_text">-</span>friendly design of <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 HE-Friendly Nucleus Sampling ‣ 3 Method ‣ Efficient Decoding Methods for Language Models on Encrypted Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sect.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
